    "turn_text": "an elected official came. to our school. I'd be on my edge of my seat listening and I always wanted to go to the state capitol and you know just those sorts of things so when I got married I told my husband hey maybe someday I might want to run for state rep but we always assumed I'd be a senior citizen and we'd after we'd had a family and they'd be all grown or whatever. So I wasn't expecting to be 33 years old and only taught school 11 years, be grading papers one night, and have the phone ring and have an elderly man in town that I'd known since I was a little girl, ask me to run for state representative. And my first thought was, no way. Politics is a dirty business and I don't want to have anything to do with it. It takes money. We don't have money. We're a farmer and a teacher. And I don't even know how to campaign. But then he said, would you think about it? Would you pray about it? And when he said that, then I had the memory of making medbys and talking to God when I was nine. I'm like, wow, maybe this is something that I should actually pray about. So I told him I would. And my husband, I sought God and we prayed, talked to others. And on the last Sunday before filing deadline, the Lord really spoke to me at church. A song came on the big screen up front that we'd sang before. But I was praying my heart, God show me if this is really what I was supposed to do. And the words actually got bold on the screen. The song was rejoicing the Lord. It said, I would join in the Lord. I will extolist love more than wine. Draw me into you and let us run together. And those words draw me into you. Let us run together. Just kind of jumped out at me and. Yeah, I knew. I was saying, show me.",
